// The round-off of the shadow lens, bounded by FPTaylor where it can be (plans/17 Ф28.D).
//
//   timeout 600 fptaylor --log-base-dir <tmp> --tmp-base-dir <tmp> --opt-x-abs-tol 0
//     --opt-x-rel-tol 0 --opt-f-rel-tol 1e-2 --opt-f-abs-tol 0 --rel-error true --abs-error false
//     --rel-error-threshold 0 shadow_lens_roundoff.fptaylor
//
// Code: crates/spacelab-propagation/src/shadow.rs, `x_minus_sin` and `lens_area`, in their f64
// operation order; every `rnd64` is one IEEE double rounding to nearest-even, no FMA. libm's sin and
// atan2 (musl's) are assumed within one ulp, stated as rnd[64, ne, 2]: a relative error of at most
// 2^-52. atan2(h, t) with h > 0 is pi/2 - atan(t/h) exactly; pi is the interval below. All bounds
// are relative; in units of u = 2^-53 they are bounds in ulp of the result, as ulp(y)/y > u.
//
// Tasks series_0 .. series_60: `x_minus_sin`'s series branch, x below 2, over x in [1, 2] and the
// dyadic boxes [2^-j, 2^-(j-1)]. One box cannot hold (0, 2]: the quotient by x - sin x ~ x^3/6
// loses its lower bound at 0. x below 2^-60 is not covered. The bound is of the twelve-term sum
// against its own real value; the tail it drops is at most x^27/27! over x^3/6 (1 - x^2/20), 1.2e-20
// relative at x = 2 (Maxima), and does not show. Task branch: x - sin x for x in [2, 2 pi].
//
// Result: the series within 1.519e-15 = 13.7u (box [1/8, 1/4]), the branch within 2.036e-16 =
// 1.83u. The test `x_minus_sin_is_within_two_ulp_of_the_reference` holds 2 ulp on its table only:
// at x = 1.858598579272284 the series returns 0.8997285676743384, 4.44 ulp from x - sin x =
// 0.89972856767433790..., replayed in Julia (the branch is + - * / only, so bit for bit) and in
// Maxima rounding each operation of the sum in rationals. Its partial sums exceed 1 while the result
// is below 1, so they round at twice the result's ulp.
//
// Tasks lens_p2 .. lens_p5: `lens_area` for the 400 km pair of tests/shadow_lens.rs (r_E and r_S,
// the doubles below, written exactly) over boxes of d whose ends are the test's grid points or the
// point where the sun's segment angle is 1.9. The angle crosses 2, the branch point, in
// the penumbra: lens_p2 .. lens_p4 take the direct branch, lens_p5 the series, and the two overlap
// where the computed angle can fall either side. Toward either tangency FPTaylor gives no useful
// bound: the sqrt of a vanishing product has an unbounded derivative. In trial runs the box from
// 1.2208848 to 1.2220462 gave 5.2e6u and the box from 1.2289832 to 1.2301834 overflowed inside
// FPTaylor, so neither is here.
//
// Result against the test's gate c1 + c2 kappa ulp of A (c1 = 4, c2 = 1, kappa = 4K/A the area's
// condition number in d, at the box ends):
//
//   box        d                       kappa          gate (ulp)     FPTaylor
//   lens_p2    1.2220462 .. 1.2232089  119 .. 181     123 .. 185     2.729e-14 = 246u
//   lens_p3    1.2232089 .. 1.2255341  181 .. 336     185 .. 340     5.623e-14 = 506u
//   lens_p4    1.2255341 .. 1.2282334  336 .. 898     340 .. 902     1.271e-13 = 1144u
//   lens_p5    1.2278593 .. 1.2289832  745 .. 1491    749 .. 1495    1.877e-13 = 1690u
//
// FPTaylor's own lower estimate is within 0.7 % of each bound, so each bound is the maximum of its
// first-order model, 1.13 to 1.49 times the gate at the box's far end. The model lets every
// rounding that acts like a shift of d take its worst sign at once: the sum r_E + r_S, as the gate's
// c2 counts, and also d - diff, d + diff, diff sum, d^2 and d^2 -+ squares. So the gate is not proved
// here. On this pair it holds where sampled: the shipped code at 40 000 random d across the penumbra
// comes to at most 0.47 of it. On three other pairs of the test it does not: up to 1.195 times the
// gate, at d the grid misses (docs/verification.md, section 1.2). c1 counts 2 ulp for x - sin x and
// no growth of atan2's one-ulp error through x - sin x.

{
Variables
  float64 x in [1, 2]
;
Definitions
  x2 = rnd64(x * x),
  t1 = rnd64(rnd64(x * x2) / 6),
  t2 = rnd64(t1 * rnd64(-x2 / 20)), s2 = rnd64(t1 + t2),
  t3 = rnd64(t2 * rnd64(-x2 / 42)), s3 = rnd64(s2 + t3),
  t4 = rnd64(t3 * rnd64(-x2 / 72)), s4 = rnd64(s3 + t4),
  t5 = rnd64(t4 * rnd64(-x2 / 110)), s5 = rnd64(s4 + t5),
  t6 = rnd64(t5 * rnd64(-x2 / 156)), s6 = rnd64(s5 + t6),
  t7 = rnd64(t6 * rnd64(-x2 / 210)), s7 = rnd64(s6 + t7),
  t8 = rnd64(t7 * rnd64(-x2 / 272)), s8 = rnd64(s7 + t8),
  t9 = rnd64(t8 * rnd64(-x2 / 342)), s9 = rnd64(s8 + t9),
  t10 = rnd64(t9 * rnd64(-x2 / 420)), s10 = rnd64(s9 + t10),
  t11 = rnd64(t10 * rnd64(-x2 / 506)), s11 = rnd64(s10 + t11),
  t12 = rnd64(t11 * rnd64(-x2 / 600)), s12 = rnd64(s11 + t12)
;
Expressions
  series_0 = s12
;
}
